  "query_preview": "..Today...Overnight convection across far northern \nArizona is giving way to clearing skies across the remainder of \nthe region. Given this trend, daytime heating looks to be able to \nmaximize destabilization over the area later today. 14Z RAP \nguidance shows a large area of 1000-2000 J/Kg of MLCAPE forecast \nfrom areas Coconino and Yavapai counties. At the same time, the \napproaching trough from the west will increase vertical wind shear\nacross the area, especially to the north. 0-6 km shear values of \naround 20-30 kts look probable across western and far northern \nArizona. As a result, strong to severe thunderstorms are expected \nto develop this afternoon across northern Arizona, with the best \nchances being over Coconino and Yavapai counties. The main threat \nwith any severe storm will be large hail, however some damaging \nwind gusts will be possible over the lower elevations.\n\nDeep monsoonal moisture is also in place across the region, with \nPWATs around an inch in the Four Corners region, increasing to \naround 1.5 inches south of the Mogollon Rim. Therefore, flash \nflooding will remain a possibility this afternoon (in addition to \nthe severe threat). The only limiting factor for flash flooding \nlooks to be 15-25 kt storm motions, though the likelihood of training \nstorms will likely negate this factor.\n\nTonight...Following today's convection across southern Arizona, an \nMCS looks to move northward out of the Lower Deserts into northern \nArizona late this evening. While the severe threat will diminish \nwith the loss of daytime heating, the flash flood potential will \nbegin to ramp up. Latest U of A WRF guidance shows the complex \nmoving up through the Phoenix metro into Yavapai and Gila counties \nafter 8-9 PM MST, then lifting northeast as it begins to decay \nthrough the rest of the overnight hours.\n\n12Z HREF PMM of >= 1\" of rain in 3 hours signals towards the \ngreatest risk areas for flash flooding overnight being across \nYavapai and Gila counties. For this reason, a Flash Flood Watch is \nin effect through the overnight hours.\n\nSunday and Monday...Monsoonal moisture looks to remain in place \nthrough Monday, along with the dynamics of the approaching trough. \nGiven this, a repeat of Saturday looks likely on Sunday and \npotentially again on Monday. Therefore, severe weather will remain a \nthreat during the afternoon hours, then transitioning to an \novernight flash flood risk as a decaying MCS moves northward out of \nthe Lower Deserts. Guidance from the U of A WRF signals towards \npotentially more widespread nocturnal activity Sunday night into \nMonday morning. HREF PMMs show an even higher signal for heavy \nrainfall across central Arizona late Sunday evening into Monday \nmorning. For this reason, an additional Flash Flood Watch has been \nissued for all of northern and central Arizona.\n\nThe limiting favor for all of this each day will be any remnant \ndebris clouds. Given the increased nocturnal activity, daytime \nheating may be limited during the morning hours. This could \npotentially result in delayed convective initiation and/or reduced \ncoverage.\n\nTuesday through Friday...A fast-moving trough looks to move through \nGreat Basin near the middle of next week. This looks to initially \nsuppress monsoonal moisture further south, with much of the GEFS, EPS, \nand CMCE member showing a sharp decline in PWATs for the middle of \nnext week. Operational guidance seems to have trended further \nwestward with Tropical Storm Karina, thereby limiting the northward \npush of the sub-tropical moisture surge along Baja California.\n\nHowever, with another tropical system potentially developing behind \nKarina and troughing remaining over the west, an increase in \nmoisture later in the week remains a possibility. GEFS, EPS, and \nCMCE members remain split on continuing with the downward trend, or \nincreasing PWATs potentially as early as Thursday/Friday. Therefore, \nuncertainty remains high near the end of the forecast package as the \nexact timing and track of the both the trough and potential tropical \ncyclone are crucial.\n\nThe operational guidance does have heights tick upwards slightly, so \na slight warming trend seems likely, but overall we look to remain \nseasonable to slightly cooler than normal temperature wise into next \nweek.",
